        "original_id": "b3241bb8-bf43-5281-8a18-f8978c8b2dfe",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- Plants: Paulownia, Dendromecon\n- Animals: Endoclita excrescens\n- Places: California, northern Baja California\n- Concepts: taxonomy, food plants\n\nFacts:\n- Paulownia is a genus of flowering plants in the family Paulowniaceae, and taxonomic authorities recognize from six to 17 species in the genus.\n- Paulownia is related to Scrophulariaceae and is sometimes included in that family.\n- Dendromecon, known as the tree poppy, is a genus of one or two species of shrubs to small trees.\n- Dendromecon is native to California and northern Baja California.\n- Endoclita excrescens feeds on plants including Castanea, Nicotiana, Paulownia, Quercus, and Raphanus.",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
      {
        "id": 1,
        "original_id": "bcf3a5db-35ce-56a8-a862-06b3e743d3ae",
        "text": "Page: Paulownia. Sentence: Paulownia is a genus of six to 17 species (depending on taxonomic authority) of flowering plants in the family Paulowniaceae, related to and sometimes included in the Scrophulariaceae.\nPage: Dendromecon. Sentence: Dendromecon, the tree poppy, is a genus of one or two species of shrubs to small trees, native to California and northern Baja California.\nPage: Endoclita excrescens. Sentence: Food plants for this species include \"Castanea\", \"Nicotiana\", \"Paulownia\", \"Quercus\", and \"Raphanus\".",
